(* ============================================================================
   PART 10: HTML SERIALIZATION CONVENTIONS (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   These conventions define the exact bytes the corpus pins. On any
   disagreement the corpus wins.

   1. ATTRIBUTE ORDER. Attributes serialize in the AUTHOR's source order
      (the recorded `order`; see the RENDER ORDER rule in PART 4 and
      PART 9 §12-style note). An Attrs built programmatically with no
      recorded order falls back to a fixed `class`, then `id`, then
      key=value order. All classes accumulate (space-joined) into one
      `class` slot at its first-appearance position. An element with no
      effective attributes emits none. (A top-level heading's id moves
      to <section> and its other attributes do not, §13; with the
      `sections` option off, or for a heading inside a container, the id
      stays on the <h*> with the rest.) A mandatory base class (math `math inline|display`) is
      prepended to that class slot.

      A STRUCTURAL ATTRIBUTE LEADS. An attribute the element derives from
      its OWN marker or destination is not "generated" in the sense below:
      it is part of the element's shape rather than something added on top
      of what the author wrote. `<ol>`'s `type` and `start` (fixed by the
      first item's marker, §11 N2), a link's `href`, an image's `src` and
      `alt` are all structural. They are emitted BEFORE the authored
      attributes, and the authored order then follows unchanged:
        > {k=v .attr}  -> <ol type="a" k="v" class="attr">
        > a. alpha
      A structural CLASS instead merges into the class slot at its FRONT,
      which is the mandatory-base-class rule above said from the other
      side (`task-list`, math's `math inline|display`).
      This matches reference djot on every shape tried --
      `<ol type="a" class="attr">`, `<ol start="5" class="attr">`,
      `<a href="…" class="c" id="i" k="v">`, `<img alt="a" src="…"
      class="c">`, `<ul class="task-list c">` -- and it is the same
      placement PART 10 §1 already states for a mention, where the
      structural `href` precedes the author's `data-role` and `id`.
      SO THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THREE-WAY, not two: structural leads,
      authored follows in source order, engine-minted follows that.

      A GENERATED ATTRIBUTE JOINS AT THE END. The two rules above cover
      an all-authored Attrs and an all-programmatic one; the MIXED case
      is a generated attribute added to attributes the author wrote, and
      it takes the authored order first, then the generated attribute.
      In practice this is one attribute -- the auto-slug id an unwrapped
      heading carries (§13):
        > {a=b .c}     -> <h1 a="b" class="c" id="Auto">
        > # Auto
      An attribute the author WROTE keeps its authored position even
      when it is the id, because it is not generated:
        > {#x a=b}      -> <h1 id="x" a="b">
        > # Written
      So the discriminator is provenance, not the attribute's name: the
      author's own order is never rearranged, and anything the engine
      minted follows it. Pinned by the corpus.

      A RENDER ANNOTATION IS EMITTED LAST -- after the generated
      attribute, not merely after the authored ones. The one such
      annotation in the core is `data-source-line` (§13-adjacent, the
      `sourceLine` render option), which records where a block was
      written rather than describing the element:
        > ## Nested   -> <h2 id="Nested" data-source-line="1">
      This is a THIRD category, and stating it is what stops an engine
      from being conformant and divergent at once. An engine that
      appends the stamp at RENDER time gets the order for free, because the
      stamp is written outside the attribute walk; an engine that attaches it
      at PARSE time carries it inside the authored run, where the "generated
      last" rule alone would put the id behind it.

      TEXT-BLOCK ALIGNMENT IS A SEMANTIC ATTRIBUTE. On `<p>`, `<div>` and
      `<h1>` through `<h6>`, an authored `align` key whose trimmed,
      ASCII-case-folded value is `left`, `right` or `center` serializes as
      `style="text-align: VALUE;"`, not as the deprecated `align` attribute.
      The declaration takes the authored `align` slot. If the author also
      supplied `style`, emit one `style` attribute at its authored slot and
      append the declaration there; the appended declaration wins if that
      style already named `text-align`.

      The element and value sets are closed. On `<table>`, `align` means block
      placement rather than alignment of cell text, so it remains an ordinary
      attribute. Every element outside the text-block set, and every value
      outside the three named values, likewise keeps the authored attribute.
      Rewriting a table's placement as `text-align` would change the document,
      not modernize an equivalent spelling.

   2. ATTRIBUTE QUOTING + ESCAPING. Attribute values are double-quoted.
      In an attribute value escape `&`->&amp;, `<`->&lt;, `>`->&gt;,
      `"`->&quot;, `'`->&apos;. In text/content escape `&`,`<`,`>` (NOT
      quotes). No other entities are produced. This describes only the
      QUOTING of values that survive filtering: dangerous attribute names,
      values, and URL schemes are dropped/blanked FIRST per PART 9 §25.

   3. VOID ELEMENTS are written WITHOUT a trailing slash: `<hr>`,
      `<img …>`, `<br>`. A hard break serializes as `<br>` + newline.

   4. BLOCK WHITESPACE. Block elements are newline-separated, one per
      line. Nested block structures (list, blockquote, table, admonition,
      div, figure, section, endnotes) indent their children by TWO spaces
      per nesting level. Inline content stays flat on its block's line.

      AN EMPTY CONTAINER BODY -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-001]. A container whose body renders
      nothing keeps a BLANK LINE where the body would be:

        <blockquote>

        </blockquote>

      with one exception, and it is an exception rather than a rule: a BARE
      `:::` div -- no type word, no class -- closes on the next line instead.

        <div>
        </div>

      So the four shapes are, exhaustively: bare div compact; div with a word
      class, admonition, and blockquote all with the blank line. A body that
      renders to nothing is an empty body -- a container holding only a comment
      takes the same shape as one holding nothing.

   5. PARAGRAPH WRAPPING follows the tight/loose rule (§17): a tight list
      item's text has no <p>; a loose item's paragraphs are wrapped.

   6. CODE. A fenced code block is `<pre><code[ class="language-X"]>` +
      escaped content + a trailing newline + `</code></pre>`. An inline
      code span is `<code>` + escaped content + `</code>`.

   7. TABLES. Alignment renders as `style="text-align: VALUE;"` (one
      space after the colon, trailing semicolon); a cell with no effective
      alignment emits NO style attribute. `rowspan`/`colspan` are emitted
      only when their count is > 1.
      A ROW IS A ROW, IN EVERY SECTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-002]. `<thead>`,
      `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>` are each written on their own lines with ONE ROW
      PER LINE, indented one level inside the section:

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">a</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

   8. STABLE STRINGS. Mentions and tags render as inert `<span>`s (no link)
      when no URL template is configured; a configured template links to them. The footnote
      backlink glyph is `↩` (§16). Math wraps content in `\(…\)` /
      `\[…\]` inside `<span class="math inline|display">` (§18).

   9. SEMANTIC SPAN ATTRIBUTES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-003]. On an ordinary `[content]{attrs}`
      span, three attribute names are reserved HTML rendering sugar: `abbr`,
      `time` and `kbd`. Each present name is consumed as an attribute and wraps
      the rendered inline content in its same-named element.

        `Press [Tab]{kbd} to indent.`        ->  `<kbd>Tab</kbd>`
        `[HTML]{abbr="HyperText Markup Language"}`
                                             ->  `<abbr title="HyperText Markup Language">HTML</abbr>`
        `[now]{time="2026-01-01"}`           ->  `<time datetime="2026-01-01">now</time>`

      A non-empty `abbr` or `time` value becomes `title` or `datetime`; a value
      on `kbd` only selects the wrapper. A DERIVED ATTRIBUTE YIELDS TO AN
      AUTHORED ONE OF THE SAME NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-004]. `title` and `datetime` are
      ordinary attribute names an author may also write, so
      `[x]{abbr="gen" title="authored"}` is `<abbr title="authored">`, not two
      `title` attributes on one element. The author wrote that one in the
      output's own vocabulary; the derived one is a convenience for the case
      where they did not. Several names nest in this fixed
      INNER-TO-OUTER order, independent of the order the author typed them:
      `abbr`, `time`, `kbd`.

      LEFTOVER ATTRIBUTES RIDE THE OUTERMOST SEMANTIC ELEMENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-005].
      Where a semantic name is present, the span does not survive as a wrapper:
      every remaining id, class and key/value attribute lands on the outermost
      semantic element and passes through §1-2 plus PART 9 §25 hardening
      there.

        `[Tab]{#k .key kbd}`    ->  `<kbd id="k" class="key">Tab</kbd>`
        `[x]{#i time="12:00" kbd}`
                                 ->  `<kbd id="i"><time datetime="12:00">x</time></kbd>`
        `[x]{kbd onclick="e"}`   ->  `<kbd>x</kbd>`
        `[x]{}`                  ->  `<span>x</span>`
        `[x]{onclick="e"}`       ->  `<span>x</span>`

      PART 9 §25 is an ATTRIBUTE policy: it removes attributes
      and never the element the author wrote, so a span with no semantic name
      renders its `<span>` whether or not anything survives hardening, and
      corpus `108-security-hardening-8` and `-10` pin those bytes. A semantic
      name is not an attribute that was removed - it never reaches the output
      as one. It RENAMES the element the author wrote, and the author's
      attributes ride the element they were written on rather than a wrapper
      the author did not write.

      THE REGISTRY HOLDS NO ELEMENT CARVE ALREADY SPELLS INLINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-006].
      A name is reserved only where the language offers no OTHER INLINE
      SPELLING for that element -- no second way to wrap a run of text where it
      stands. `code` and `mark` do not qualify: a code span (`` `x` ``) writes
      `<code>` and the highlight syntax (`=x=`) writes `<mark>`, each the same
      authoring gesture on the same run. (The inline literal `` !`x` `` is not
      a third spelling of `<code>`: §27 drops the wrapper, which is the point
      of it.) `code` made the cost concrete rather than merely untidy: a code
      span is VERBATIM -- `` `*b*` `` is `<code>*b*</code>` -- while an
      extension body is parsed, so `:code[*b*]` produced
      `<code><strong>b</strong></code>`, one tag with two content models,
      selected by which spelling the author reached for and reported by
      nothing.

      THE TEST IS THE SPELLING, NOT THE TAG, and `abbr` is why it has to be
      said. A document-level `*[HTML]: …` definition also emits `<abbr>`, and
      that does NOT disqualify the name: a definition is a different mechanism
      at a different level. It expands EVERY occurrence of its term
      automatically and document-wide, and cannot mark one.
      `[HTML]{abbr="Custom"}` marks exactly one, with its own title, while the
      definition keeps expanding the rest; `[WHO]{abbr="World Health
      Organization"}` marks a term no definition declares; `[HTML]{abbr}` marks
      an abbreviation with no expansion at all. None of those is writable with
      `*[…]:`.

      THE CORE SEMANTIC NAMES ARE CLOSED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-007]. A name
      is core when it preserves otherwise-lost authored data or a core clause
      already governs its interaction. Core recognizes exactly `abbr`, `time`,
      and `kbd`; `kbd` is also retained for its broad use across comparable
      systems. `samp`, `var`, `cite`, and `dfn` meet neither criterion and
      belong to the SemanticSpan extension described in §10.

      An explicit `abbr` attribute is authoritative over automatic abbreviation
      expansion inside that span. A resolved `abbreviation` child contributes
      only its visible abbreviation text there; renderers MUST NOT emit a
      nested `<abbr>`. Thus a document-level `*[HTML]: Long` definition does not
      override `[HTML]{abbr="Custom"}`.

      This rule changes only HTML rendering. The AST remains an ordinary `span`
      carrying the authored attributes, plain and ANSI targets render its
      inline content, and the canonical writer emits the span with its
      attributes, a value-less one spelled bare per PART 11 §6c.

      Accessible ruby is NOT reserved here: it requires structured
      base/annotation children rather than a single inline container.

   10. THE OTHER SEMANTIC NAMES ARE AN EXTENSION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-008]. `samp`, `var`,
      `cite` and `dfn` carry no data and collide with no core clause, so they
      are not core: a conformant core renders `[x]{samp}` as an ordinary
      boolean attribute (`<span samp="">x</span>`) and `:samp[x]` through the
      generic `<span class="ext-samp">` fallback.

      They are the `SemanticSpan` extension, Tier-2 and off by default, whose
      contract is `docs/extensions.md` §8: when it is enabled the four names
      behave exactly as §9's three do, under the same nesting order, the same
      value mapping for `dfn`, the same attribute riding, and the same
      hardening. The extension accepts `:name[…]` for all seven names as a
      soft-deprecated form scheduled for removal in 0.2; writers use the
      compact spelling.

      A core implementation MUST NOT turn an arbitrary extension name into an
      HTML tag: outside `SemanticSpan`, every `:name[…]` keeps the generic
      fallback. `cite` denotes a work title or source and is unrelated to a
      bibliographic `[@key]` citation, which is the Citations extension's.

   11. AN EXTENDED TASK STATE NAMES ITSELF ON THE ITEM -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-009].
      PART 2 admits seven task states and §2's box has two
      positions, so `[-]`, `[_]`, `[>]` and `[?]` rendered bytes identical to
      `[ ]`: the distinction the author made survived the file (PART 11 §6g)
      and died at the renderer, out of reach of any stylesheet. The item
      carries `data-task-state` holding the authored character:

        - [-] dropped   ->  <li data-task-state="-"><input type="checkbox"
                            disabled aria-label="dropped"> dropped</li>

      ONLY WHERE THE AST RECORDS A STATE, which is the same rule PART 12
      states for the field: `[ ]` and `[x]` carry no attribute, because the
      box already says which they are, and their bytes are unchanged by this
      clause. An item whose `taskState` is absent emits nothing here.

      ON THE `<li>`, NOT ON THE `<input>`. The state is a property of the
      ITEM -- PART 2 says so of the checkbox itself -- and a stylesheet
      reaching the item's own text is what the attribute is for
      (`li[data-task-state="-"]`). Putting it on the box would put the
      selector one level below the content it wants to reach.

      IT IS A STRUCTURAL ATTRIBUTE and leads the authored ones (§1), like
      `<ol>`'s `type` and a link's `href`: the item derives it from its own
      marker rather than from anything the author attached.

      THE VALUE IS THE RECORDED CHARACTER, escaped as §2 escapes any value --
      `>` is written `&gt;`, and a value outside PART 2's enumeration is not
      one this clause writes.

      AN IMPORTER CONSUMES IT, and consuming is not the same as dropping a
      DERIVED attribute (PART 9 §16a). The checkbox `<input>` is already
      consumed into the item's state rather than kept as content; this
      attribute is the other half of that state and is read the same way. It
      is what lets a document survive a render-and-import cycle: without it
      `[-]` comes back as `[ ]`, silently, because both spellings render the
      same box and no diagnostic can see a difference that is not there.

      THE ACCESSIBLE NAME DOES NOT MOVE. The box keeps the item's own text as
      its `aria-label` (PART 2). Naming the state there would invent copy the
      author did not write, and the states are an author's shorthand rather
      than a vocabulary this spec can translate.


   ============================================================================ *)
